import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { specificationDoc, implementationDoc } from '../../data/coordinates';
---

<Base
  title="Linked Data CRUD in sempods — resources with their own addresses"
  description="Read and write JSON-LD resources over HTTP. The current contract separates a resource's URI from its selected write Context."
  schemaType="TechArticle"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">concepts · HTTP</p>
  <h1>The resource keeps its address</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An event's URI identifies the event and gives clients a place to read
    about it. Apps can use ordinary HTTP operations on linked data that
    exists outside any one SDK.
  </p>
  <h2>Read a resource</h2>
  <p>
    Request the URI and receive the description you may read, usually as
    JSON-LD. Reads can merge statements from readable Contexts. Anonymous
    callers receive public data; authenticated callers may receive more.
  </p>
  <p>
    A client can narrow the read to selected Contexts. Graph-aware
    representations can preserve Context grouping when provenance matters.
  </p>
  <h2>Write into an explicit Context</h2>
  <p>
    In the current contract, each write selects one Context. The resource
    IRI says which thing is described; the Context identifies the statements
    the caller is authorized to change. A replacement affects that resource's
    outgoing statements within the selected Context.
  </p>
  <p>
    Conditional operations help a client avoid overwriting a change it has
    not read. The contract also exposes property slots and edges with their
    own operation rules.
  </p>
  <h2>Control-plane authority has its own routes</h2>
  <p>
    Grants and registrations are protected control-plane state. Writing RDF
    about an identity or permission does not grant authority. Their management
    goes through explicit APIs under <code>/_system</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The specification and implementation also protect reserved namespaces.
    Check the contract and known deviations before treating a control-plane
    IRI as an ordinary write target.
  </p>
  <h2>A client library makes the path easier</h2>
  <p>
    Kotlin 0.2 separates the HTTP client from optional RDF4J and media adapters.
    The HTTP core handles JSON-LD text and bytes, with endpoint groups for
    resources, subjects, slots, Contexts, SPARQL and metadata.
  </p>
  <p>
    The wire contract stays inspectable. You can choose another library or
    write a client in another language.
  </p>
  <p class="action-links">
    <a href={specificationDoc('spec/core/lod-crud.md')}>CRUD contract</a>{' '}
    <a href={implementationDoc('sempods-client/README.md')}>JVM client guide</a>{' '}
    <a href="/start#build">Read a resource with the client</a>
  </p>
</Base>
